WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:06.160
The Vision Pro is dead, but it's&nbsp;
way worse than everyone realizes.&nbsp;

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:10.720
In 2024, the Vision Pro sold&nbsp;
around 390 thousand units.&nbsp;

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:13.440
The next year, the holiday quarter — one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:21.120
biggest of the year — was just 45 thousand.
And the lifetime sales… are even more depressing.&nbsp;

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:26.560
Now, Apple has cut the marketing budget&nbsp;
by more than 95%, halted production,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:31.200
and cancelled the newest version.
Despite the high price, every Vision&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:36.640
Pro was costing Apple even more money.
Why did this thing fail so badly?&nbsp;

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:52.000
And, what is Apple doing now?
This might be Apple's biggest disaster, ever.

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:17.440
The Vision Pro should have worked.&nbsp;

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:22.240
It ticked all the boxes in Apple's&nbsp;
foolproof, tried and true strategy.&nbsp;

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:26.320
Well… except one.
So what was it?

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:33.520
To find out, we need to look at Apple's Playbook.
The iPod, iPhone, iPad, and Apple Watch followed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:39.840
a similar three-step strategy: enter&nbsp;
late, polish hard, and dominate.&nbsp;

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:44.240
But this failed with the Vision&nbsp;
Pro, which lacked a crucial detail.&nbsp;

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:48.560
When the iPhone debuted, it was released at two&nbsp;
price points: $499 for the 4GB model and $599&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:54.160
for the 8GB model.
Compared to the BlackBerry Storm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.160 --> 00:02:00.240
which cost $199, and the Nokia 6300 or 5310&nbsp;
XpressMusic, which typically cost between $150&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:07.760
and $300, the iPhone was far more expensive.
But… it was the iPhone, and people were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:13.680
lining up for as long as a week to get it.
The Apple Watch was similar. Starting at $359&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:19.280
and $549. It did have a $10,000 version…&nbsp;
which Apple ditched the following year.&nbsp;

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:25.120
The iPad was $499 in 2010.
Apple products aren't really new.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:30.240
They come into the game when competitors are&nbsp;
everywhere, and they come out more expensive.&nbsp;

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:34.320
They more so "iterate" on what&nbsp;
exists, rather than invent.

00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:13.600
But, if you want a new technology to become&nbsp;
mainstream, wouldn't you make it as affordable as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:19.440
possible? And wouldn't this help the Vision Pro?
Many other companies do this, often taking a loss&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:22.800
to get as much market penetration&nbsp;
as possible, for lower costs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:28.480
and in the case of tech: developer support.
This is why the Meta Quest is so cheap,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:32.880
but more on that later.
Apple… often takes a very different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:38.160
approach, and for a very important reason.
Apple starts high, targeting customers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:42.960
who are willing to pay the most.&nbsp;
Luxury customers and diehard fans.&nbsp;

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:46.640
Then, as production gets cheaper&nbsp;
and the technology matures, Apple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:52.800
expands. But, it doesn't really get cheaper.
They make more versions, more price variants,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:58.080
a bit cheaper, but also more expensive.
It moves from premium to a more accessible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:02.800
broader market, but still "feels"&nbsp;
more premium than competitors.&nbsp;

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:09.520
But, this is where the Vision Pro is a bit weird.
It seems to tick all the boxes. It entered late,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.520 --> 00:04:13.520
it iterated on VR with new&nbsp;
technology, but didn't invent it.&nbsp;

00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:17.920
It had full ecosystem integration,&nbsp;
running iPhone and iPad apps.&nbsp;

00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:21.600
But, Apple overlooked one&nbsp;
crucial part of their playbook.&nbsp;

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:26.640
There wasn't a big VR market.
All their successful products enter late,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:32.480
because they have huge market validation.
There were smartphones before the iPhone, and many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:37.280
mobile phones. Heaps of tablets before the iPad.
Did they redefine those markets after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:40.800
entering? Absolutely.
But, the most important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:45.600
thing is that Apple waited until there&nbsp;
were clear customers spending money here.&nbsp;

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:52.080
With VR, there were plenty of products, but&nbsp;
the number of customers was super niche.&nbsp;

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:58.160
And even today, in 2026, the market is&nbsp;
shrinking, falling by 14% year-over-year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:02.720
while Meta's cheaper Quest at $500&nbsp;
controls around 80% of the market.&nbsp;

00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:07.360
But, there was another small problem,&nbsp;
which makes all of this much stranger.

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:12.640
That price.

00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:17.680
For decades, Apple has been able to charge premium&nbsp;
prices because consumers are willing to pay extra&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:23.440
for what they perceive as the best version.
This time, there wasn't a mass market for Apple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:26.480
to position this high.
The Meta Quest 3,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.480 --> 00:05:32.960
at just $500, with the 2 as low as $300.
Even the premium Quest Pro was just $999.

00:05:32.960 --> 00:05:38.320
Apple products are usually much more&nbsp;
expensive, but nothing like this.&nbsp;

00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:41.360
$3,499.
So the Vision&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:47.280
Pro had a fatal flaw, though it still could&nbsp;
have been a more niche Apple product, right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:52.560
like the Pro display XDR.
Well, not necessarily.&nbsp;

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:57.120
The unit economics of this is crazy.
The amount of money Apple poured into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.120 --> 00:06:10.480
this thing has caused the Vision Pro to&nbsp;
be the worst Apple disaster in decades.

00:06:10.480 --> 00:06:15.280
To understand how bad things really are,&nbsp;
let's take a closer look at those sales.

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:20.560
Vision Pro launched ok, shipping&nbsp;
around 390,000 units in 2024.&nbsp;

00:06:20.560 --> 00:06:27.200
But by the 2025 holiday season? One of the biggest&nbsp;
spending quarters of the year? 45,000 units.&nbsp;

00:06:27.200 --> 00:06:31.680
For context, the Mac shipped 7.1&nbsp;
million in that single quarter.&nbsp;

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:37.040
Also, it’s still only available in 13 countries.
Even in the US, which is Apple's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.040 --> 00:06:41.600
strongest market, it's bad.
Stores are discounting it.&nbsp;

00:06:41.600 --> 00:06:45.440
Apple has been trying to revive&nbsp;
it with new features, more apps,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.440 --> 00:06:51.840
and even a potential cheaper version, the&nbsp;
Vision Air. But none of it moved the needle.&nbsp;

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:59.040
So in April, Apple halted production altogether.
Now here's the thing. Apple makes expensive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.040 --> 00:07:04.320
products. And some of them struggle. But&nbsp;
Apple, usually eventually, breaks even,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:09.520
or better, because they sell huge volumes.
But with the Vision Pro, the fundamental&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.520 --> 00:07:13.040
structure is broken. Here's why.
The Real Problem&nbsp;

00:07:13.040 --> 00:07:18.240
When the Vision Pro launched, it was already the&nbsp;
most expensive headset ever, by a factor of three.&nbsp;

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:22.640
Estimates put the cost of&nbsp;
R&amp;D at about 20-33 billion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.640 --> 00:07:28.400
which lines up with what Meta spent on R&amp;D too.
For comparison, Apple spent fifteen years and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.400 --> 00:07:34.240
roughly $10 billion on its cancelled car project,&nbsp;
far less than what Vision Pro may have cost.

00:07:34.240 --> 00:07:38.800
And that's where this headset starts to look&nbsp;
less like a premium product… and more like an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.800 --> 00:07:42.160
expensive problem for Apple.
But, it gets worse when we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.160 --> 00:07:46.240
look at the variable costs.
The cost of each Vision Pro.

00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:50.080
Research firm Omdia estimated the&nbsp;
Vision Pro's bill of materials at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.080 --> 00:07:58.240
$1,542. That's excluding R&amp;D, packaging, and&nbsp;
marketing. Just the raw materials and parts.&nbsp;

00:07:58.240 --> 00:08:03.760
"The most expensive part in the headset is the&nbsp;
1.25 inch Sony Semiconductor display that goes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.760 --> 00:08:09.920
in front of the user's eye… Apple pays about&nbsp;
$228 for the "Micro OLED" displays it uses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.920 --> 00:08:15.600
according to the Omdia estimate. Each Vision&nbsp;
Pro needs two of them, one for each eye."&nbsp;

00:08:15.600 --> 00:08:19.520
At $1,500, the selling price&nbsp;
begins to make more sense.&nbsp;

00:08:19.520 --> 00:08:24.560
With a $3,499 retail price, the Vision&nbsp;
Pro sells for about 2.3 times the cost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.560 --> 00:08:29.920
of the parts inside it, and puts the&nbsp;
gross profit margin at about 56%.&nbsp;

00:08:29.920 --> 00:08:35.840
That’s pretty standard for Apple, but,&nbsp;
that's just the gross profit. Net profit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.840 --> 00:08:42.480
including marketing and most of all that&nbsp;
gigantic R&amp;D cost, makes things much worse.&nbsp;

00:08:42.480 --> 00:08:46.640
Even assuming the lower end, Apple&nbsp;
spent around $20 billion developing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.640 --> 00:08:53.120
Vision Pro and sold roughly 600,000 units.
That works out to more than $33,000 in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.120 --> 00:08:59.920
development costs for every headset sold.
While, Vision Pro retailed for $3,499.&nbsp;

00:08:59.920 --> 00:09:08.080
So, including the material cost and the price,&nbsp;
Apple lost, on average, $28,000 per Vision Pro.

00:09:08.080 --> 00:09:12.960
It's probably not exactly that, but…&nbsp;
it's definitely around this area.&nbsp;

00:09:12.960 --> 00:09:17.440
Apple had to price it high because of&nbsp;
its usual rulebook, and the high costs.&nbsp;

00:09:17.440 --> 00:09:20.640
But, that high price meant&nbsp;
it stayed a niche product.&nbsp;

00:09:20.640 --> 00:09:23.280
Now, Apple is basically giving up.&nbsp;

00:09:23.280 --> 00:09:27.040
They've halted production from Apple's&nbsp;
manufacturing partner, Luxshare.

00:09:27.040 --> 00:09:32.480
They've cut advertising by more than 95%.
They halted work on the new version:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.480 --> 00:09:36.480
the Vision Air.
I don't think Apple should have gone into VR.&nbsp;

00:09:36.480 --> 00:09:42.240
Some markets can be left on the table, and it's&nbsp;
okay to say no to a new expansion or new product.

00:09:42.800 --> 00:09:48.160
But, there's something else to this story.
Wearable tech is selling.&nbsp;

00:09:48.160 --> 00:09:53.120
But while Apple spent billions building a heavy&nbsp;
computer for your face, the real solution may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.120 --> 00:10:07.040
have been something far simpler all along.
A solution which Apple is now chasing.

00:10:07.040 --> 00:10:14.080
We’re almost at 1 million subscribers, so if you&nbsp;
haven’t already, please subscribe! Thank you.

00:10:14.080 --> 00:10:19.760
The Meta Quest line didn't really go anywhere.
Meta made a lot of the same mistakes as Apple,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.760 --> 00:10:24.880
though they tried to price low and get as&nbsp;
much developer support and market penetration.&nbsp;

00:10:24.880 --> 00:10:30.720
But, Meta wearables are succeeding now.
They've targeted the same everyday consumer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.720 --> 00:10:35.040
that Apple had in mind by building&nbsp;
something they were already used to.

00:10:56.080 --> 00:11:00.000
These are the Meta Ray-Bans, with their&nbsp;
partnership with the world's biggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:07.760
eyewear maker, released on September 17, 2025.
Ironically, Meta is using Apple's strategy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.760 --> 00:11:15.360
Entering late, iterate, polish hard, go premium.
Smart glasses and phones existed before.&nbsp;

00:11:15.360 --> 00:11:20.160
They have a heads-up display, an integrated&nbsp;
AI assistant that captures audio and video,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.160 --> 00:11:25.440
then either processes parts of that on-device or&nbsp;
hands work off to a paired phone or the cloud.&nbsp;

00:11:25.440 --> 00:11:32.560
In just 2025, Meta sold over 7 million Meta&nbsp;
AI glasses after estimating just 2 million.&nbsp;

00:11:32.560 --> 00:11:37.840
Then something interesting happened.
On October 1, 2025, Apple paused the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.840 --> 00:11:42.400
Vision Air production, the lighter version&nbsp;
of Vision Pro, to focus on accelerating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.400 --> 00:11:49.120
production on smart glasses with a display.
That came just 14 days after that Meta demo.&nbsp;

00:11:49.120 --> 00:11:54.560
Then on April 20th, Apple announced that John&nbsp;
Ternus would become the company's next CEO,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.560 --> 00:11:59.440
taking over from Tim Cook on September 1st, 2026.
Why does that matter?&nbsp;

00:11:59.440 --> 00:12:04.400
Because reports have shown that Ternus was&nbsp;
one of the biggest skeptics of the Vision Pro.&nbsp;

00:12:04.400 --> 00:12:09.680
Ternus has also approved a major overhaul of&nbsp;
Apple's headset and smart glasses roadmap,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.680 --> 00:12:12.880
which will now focus on just two smart glasses.&nbsp;

00:12:12.880 --> 00:12:19.120
Apparently, Apple is preparing to unveil smart&nbsp;
glasses as early as September or October 2026.&nbsp;

00:12:19.120 --> 00:12:25.200
So, we're likely to see something from Apple soon.
But, something really weird is happening.

00:12:25.200 --> 00:12:30.480
Meta unveiled its display glasses, and&nbsp;
just two weeks later, Apple killed its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.480 --> 00:12:34.160
cheaper Vision Pro successor.
Think about that for a second.&nbsp;

00:12:34.160 --> 00:12:37.440
For years, Apple was the company&nbsp;
setting the direction of consumer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.440 --> 00:12:42.960
technology. Everyone else followed.
The iPhone, iPad, Apple Watch.&nbsp;

00:12:42.960 --> 00:12:48.080
But this time, Apple is the follower.
The funny part is that Apple is doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.080 --> 00:12:51.360
exactly what Meta did.
They went all in on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.360 --> 00:12:57.360
revolutionary VR product, spent billions, it&nbsp;
flopped, and is now moving to smart glasses.&nbsp;

00:12:57.360 --> 00:13:04.160
So, the one problem in the "Apple roadmap" is&nbsp;
fixed. But will Apple just be chasing Meta now?&nbsp;

00:13:04.160 --> 00:13:08.240
When the Apple glasses launch,&nbsp;
assuming maybe 2027, the Meta Glasses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.240 --> 00:13:13.840
will have a few years head start over Apple.
They're already being used by millions of people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.840 --> 00:13:18.800
and this has caused another big problem&nbsp;
that not enough people are talking about.

00:13:18.800 --> 00:13:23.200
These AI glasses are a privacy&nbsp;
disaster, that's already happening.&nbsp;

00:13:23.200 --> 00:13:25.760
They're always on, always recording,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.760 --> 00:13:29.360
always listening, and privacy&nbsp;
experts are starting to panic.

00:13:29.360 --> 00:13:34.160
And, unlike smartphones or social media,&nbsp;
these glasses make it worse not just for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.160 --> 00:13:40.000
those wearing them, but everyone.
Watch this video next to learn more.

